    {
      "id": "c10-2-ex2-1-q1",
      "classNum": 10,
      "chapter": "2",
      "exercise": "2.1",
      "topic": {
        "en": "Numbers and Sequences"
      },
      "label": {
        "en": "Exercise 2.1 · Q1",
        "ta": "Exercise 2.1 · Q1 (English only; Tamil pending)"
      },
      "title": {
        "en": "Integers with remainder 2"
      },
      "q": {
        "en": "Find all positive integers, when divided by 3 leaves remainder 2."
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "en": "Use the division algorithm. A remainder of 2 on division by 3 means the integer is 3 times its quotient plus 2.",
          "eq": "n = 3k + 2"
        },
        {
          "en": "Because n is positive, the integer quotient k starts at 0. Use k = 0, 1, 2, 3 and continue.",
          "eq": "k = 0, 1, 2, 3, … ⇒ n = 2, 5, 8, 11, …"
        },
        {
          "en": "Conversely, every number of this form is positive and leaves remainder 2, which is smaller than the divisor 3.",
          "eq": "n = 3k + 2, where k is an integer and k ≥ 0"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "2, 5, 8, 11, …; all numbers n = 3k + 2, where k = 0, 1, 2, ….",
      "kind": "textbook",
      "source": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/13XJXdPhuGqU3BG3BPrsmzZwUUFOuH6Of/view",
      "reference": "TN SCERT Standard Ten Mathematics; 2025 revised English edition; Chapter 2, Exercise 2.1 Q1; printed p. 42, PDF p. 48.",
      "edition": "2025",
      "translationReview": "pending"
    },
    {
      "id": "c10-2-ex2-1-q2",
      "classNum": 10,
      "chapter": "2",
      "exercise": "2.1",
      "topic": {
        "en": "Numbers and Sequences"
      },
      "label": {
        "en": "Exercise 2.1 · Q2",
        "ta": "Exercise 2.1 · Q2 (English only; Tamil pending)"
      },
      "title": {
        "en": "Rows of flower pots"
      },
      "q": {
        "en": "A man has 532 flower pots. He wants to arrange them in rows such that each row contains 21 flower pots. Find the number of completed rows and how many flower pots are left over."
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "en": "Divide the total number of flower pots by the number required in each row.",
          "eq": "532 = 21 × 25 + 7"
        },
        {
          "en": "The quotient counts complete rows, so 25 rows each contain 21 pots.",
          "eq": "21 × 25 = 525"
        },
        {
          "en": "Subtract the pots already arranged. Seven cannot fill another row of 21.",
          "eq": "532 − 525 = 7; 0 ≤ 7 < 21"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "25 completed rows; 7 flower pots are left over.",
      "kind": "textbook",
      "source": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/13XJXdPhuGqU3BG3BPrsmzZwUUFOuH6Of/view",
      "reference": "TN SCERT Standard Ten Mathematics; 2025 revised English edition; Chapter 2, Exercise 2.1 Q2; printed p. 42, PDF p. 48.",
      "edition": "2025",
      "translationReview": "pending"
    },
    {
      "id": "c10-2-ex2-1-q3",
      "classNum": 10,
      "chapter": "2",
      "exercise": "2.1",
      "topic": {
        "en": "Numbers and Sequences"
      },
      "label": {
        "en": "Exercise 2.1 · Q3",
        "ta": "Exercise 2.1 · Q3 (English only; Tamil pending)"
      },
      "title": {
        "en": "Product of consecutive integers"
      },
      "q": {
        "en": "Prove that the product of two consecutive positive integers is divisible by 2."
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "en": "Let the consecutive positive integers be n and n+1. Every integer is either even or odd.",
          "eq": "n = 2k or n = 2k + 1"
        },
        {
          "en": "If n is even, it already contributes a factor of 2 to the product.",
          "eq": "n(n+1) = (2k)(2k+1) = 2[k(2k+1)]"
        },
        {
          "en": "If n is odd, the next integer is even and contributes a factor of 2.",
          "eq": "n(n+1) = (2k+1)(2k+2) = 2[(2k+1)(k+1)]"
        },
        {
          "en": "In both cases the bracket is an integer. Thus the product is divisible by 2.",
          "eq": "2 divides n(n+1)"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "The product of any two consecutive positive integers is divisible by 2.",
      "kind": "textbook",
      "source": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/13XJXdPhuGqU3BG3BPrsmzZwUUFOuH6Of/view",
      "reference": "TN SCERT Standard Ten Mathematics; 2025 revised English edition; Chapter 2, Exercise 2.1 Q3; printed p. 43, PDF p. 49.",
      "edition": "2025",
      "translationReview": "pending"
    },
    {
      "id": "c10-2-ex2-1-q4",
      "classNum": 10,
      "chapter": "2",
      "exercise": "2.1",
      "topic": {
        "en": "Numbers and Sequences"
      },
      "label": {
        "en": "Exercise 2.1 · Q4",
        "ta": "Exercise 2.1 · Q4 (English only; Tamil pending)"
      },
      "title": {
        "en": "Sum of three remainders"
      },
      "q": {
        "en": "When the positive integers a, b and c are divided by 13, the respective remainders are 9,7 and 10. Show that a+b+c is divisible by 13."
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "en": "Write each positive integer using its quotient and the stated remainder. The quotients p, q and r are nonnegative integers.",
          "eq": "a = 13p + 9; b = 13q + 7; c = 13r + 10"
        },
        {
          "en": "Add the three expressions and combine their remainders.",
          "eq": "a+b+c = 13(p+q+r) + (9+7+10)"
        },
        {
          "en": "The remainders sum to 26, which is itself a multiple of 13.",
          "eq": "a+b+c = 13(p+q+r) + 26 = 13(p+q+r+2)"
        },
        {
          "en": "Since p+q+r+2 is an integer, the sum is divisible by 13.",
          "eq": "13 divides a+b+c"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "a+b+c = 13(p+q+r+2), so a+b+c is divisible by 13.",
      "kind": "textbook",
      "source": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/13XJXdPhuGqU3BG3BPrsmzZwUUFOuH6Of/view",
      "reference": "TN SCERT Standard Ten Mathematics; 2025 revised English edition; Chapter 2, Exercise 2.1 Q4; printed p. 43, PDF p. 49.",
      "edition": "2025",
      "translationReview": "pending"
    },
    {
      "id": "c10-2-ex2-1-q5",
      "classNum": 10,
      "chapter": "2",
      "exercise": "2.1",
      "topic": {
