      "label": {
        "en": "Exercise 2.1 · Q5",
        "ta": "Exercise 2.1 · Q5 (English only; Tamil pending)"
      },
      "title": {
        "en": "Remainder of an integer square"
      },
      "q": {
        "en": "Prove that square of any integer leaves the remainder either 0 or 1 when divided by 4."
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "en": "Every integer n, including a negative integer or zero, is even or odd. Write it in one of these two forms with integer k.",
          "eq": "n = 2k or n = 2k + 1"
        },
        {
          "en": "If n is even, square its expression. It is exactly a multiple of 4.",
          "eq": "n² = (2k)² = 4k²"
        },
        {
          "en": "If n is odd, expand its square and separate a multiple of 4.",
          "eq": "n² = (2k+1)² = 4k²+4k+1 = 4(k²+k)+1"
        },
        {
          "en": "Both cases cover every integer. The possible remainders 0 and 1 are each between 0 and 3.",
          "eq": "n² has remainder 0 or 1 when divided by 4"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "The remainder is 0 for an even integer and 1 for an odd integer.",
      "kind": "textbook",
      "source": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/13XJXdPhuGqU3BG3BPrsmzZwUUFOuH6Of/view",
      "reference": "TN SCERT Standard Ten Mathematics; 2025 revised English edition; Chapter 2, Exercise 2.1 Q5; printed p. 43, PDF p. 49.",
      "edition": "2025",
      "translationReview": "pending"
    },
    {
      "id": "c10-2-ex2-1-q6",
      "classNum": 10,
      "chapter": "2",
      "exercise": "2.1",
      "topic": {
        "en": "Numbers and Sequences"
      },
      "label": {
        "en": "Exercise 2.1 · Q6",
        "ta": "Exercise 2.1 · Q6 (English only; Tamil pending)"
      },
      "title": {
        "en": "HCF by Euclid’s Division Algorithm"
      },
      "q": {
        "en": "Use Euclid’s Division Algorithm to find the Highest Common Factor (HCF) of (i) 340 and 412 (ii) 867 and 255 (iii)10224 and 9648 (iv) 84, 90 and 120"
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "en": "In each pair, repeatedly divide the larger number by the smaller. Replace the pair by the divisor and remainder until the remainder is zero. The last nonzero remainder is the HCF.",
          "eq": "HCF(a,b) = HCF(b,r), where a = bq+r and 0 ≤ r < b"
        },
        {
          "en": "For (i), start with 412 and 340 and continue the divisions.",
          "eq": "412 = 340 × 1 + 72; 340 = 72 × 4 + 52; 72 = 52 × 1 + 20"
        },
        {
          "en": "Finish (i). The last nonzero remainder is 4.",
          "eq": "52 = 20 × 2 + 12; 20 = 12 × 1 + 8; 12 = 8 × 1 + 4; 8 = 4 × 2 + 0"
        },
        {
          "en": "For (ii), the last nonzero remainder is 51.",
          "eq": "867 = 255 × 3 + 102; 255 = 102 × 2 + 51; 102 = 51 × 2 + 0"
        },
        {
          "en": "For (iii), first divide 10224 by 9648, then repeat with the remainders.",
          "eq": "10224 = 9648 × 1 + 576; 9648 = 576 × 16 + 432"
        },
        {
          "en": "Finish (iii). The last nonzero remainder is 144.",
          "eq": "576 = 432 × 1 + 144; 432 = 144 × 3 + 0"
        },
        {
          "en": "For (iv), first find the HCF of 84 and 90.",
          "eq": "90 = 84 × 1 + 6; 84 = 6 × 14 + 0"
        },
        {
          "en": "Now find the HCF of this result and 120. Thus the HCF of all three numbers is 6.",
          "eq": "120 = 6 × 20 + 0; HCF(84,90,120) = 6"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "(i) 4; (ii) 51; (iii) 144; (iv) 6.",
      "kind": "textbook",
      "source": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/13XJXdPhuGqU3BG3BPrsmzZwUUFOuH6Of/view",
      "reference": "TN SCERT Standard Ten Mathematics; 2025 revised English edition; Chapter 2, Exercise 2.1 Q6; printed p. 43, PDF p. 49.",
      "edition": "2025",
      "translationReview": "pending"
    },
    {
      "id": "c10-2-ex2-1-q7",
      "classNum": 10,
      "chapter": "2",
      "exercise": "2.1",
      "topic": {
        "en": "Numbers and Sequences"
      },
      "label": {
        "en": "Exercise 2.1 · Q7",
        "ta": "Exercise 2.1 · Q7 (English only; Tamil pending)"
      },
      "title": {
        "en": "Largest divisor with a fixed remainder"
      },
      "q": {
        "en": "Find the largest number which divides 1230 and 1926 leaving remainder 12 in each case."
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "en": "If a number divides each original number leaving remainder 12, it divides each number after subtracting 12.",
          "eq": "1230−12 = 1218; 1926−12 = 1914"
        },
        {
          "en": "Find the HCF of these two differences by Euclid’s Division Algorithm.",
          "eq": "1914 = 1218 × 1 + 696; 1218 = 696 × 1 + 522"
        },
        {
          "en": "Continue until the remainder is zero. The HCF is 174.",
          "eq": "696 = 522 × 1 + 174; 522 = 174 × 3 + 0"
        },
        {
          "en": "Every valid divisor is a common divisor of the differences, so none exceeds their HCF. Check that 174 itself leaves remainder 12 in both original numbers.",
          "eq": "1230 = 174 × 7 + 12; 1926 = 174 × 11 + 12; 12 < 174"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "174.",
      "kind": "textbook",
      "source": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/13XJXdPhuGqU3BG3BPrsmzZwUUFOuH6Of/view",
      "reference": "TN SCERT Standard Ten Mathematics; 2025 revised English edition; Chapter 2, Exercise 2.1 Q7; printed p. 43, PDF p. 49.",
      "edition": "2025",
      "translationReview": "pending"
    },
    {
      "id": "c10-2-ex2-1-q8",
      "classNum": 10,
      "chapter": "2",
      "exercise": "2.1",
      "topic": {
        "en": "Numbers and Sequences"
      },
      "label": {
        "en": "Exercise 2.1 · Q8",
        "ta": "Exercise 2.1 · Q8 (English only; Tamil pending)"
      },
      "title": {
        "en": "Express the HCF as a linear combination"
      },
      "q": {
        "en": "If d is the Highest Common Factor of 32 and 60, find x and y satisfying d = 32x + 60y."
      },
      "steps": [
        {
          "en": "Find d using Euclid’s Division Algorithm. The last nonzero remainder is 4.",
          "eq": "60 = 32 × 1 + 28; 32 = 28 × 1 + 4; 28 = 4 × 7 + 0"
        },
        {
          "en": "Work backwards to express the remainder 4 in terms of 32 and 28.",
          "eq": "d = 4 = 32 − 28"
        },
        {
          "en": "Replace 28 by 60−32 and collect multiples of the original numbers.",
          "eq": "4 = 32 − (60−32) = 2 × 32 − 60"
        },
        {
          "en": "Compare with d=32x+60y. This supplies one integer pair; substitute it to check.",
          "eq": "x = 2, y = −1; 32 × 2 + 60 × (−1) = 64−60 = 4"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "d = 4; one solution is x = 2, y = −1.",
      "kind": "textbook",
      "source": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/13XJXdPhuGqU3BG3BPrsmzZwUUFOuH6Of/view",
      "reference": "TN SCERT Standard Ten Mathematics; 2025 revised English edition; Chapter 2, Exercise 2.1 Q8; printed p. 43, PDF p. 49.",
      "edition": "2025",
      "translationReview": "pending"
    },
    {
